index 0000000..5e04d4f
--- /dev/null
+++ b/NETWORK_MODES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,136 @@
+# Maintained macOS network-mode API draft (0.0.74, API v1)
+
+This is a local distribution patch maintained by the Pi permissions integration,
+not an upstream release or cross-platform enforcement claim. Native validation
+and independent review are installation gates. The package version remains
+0.0.74. Do not infer support from that version or schema acceptance alone:
+pristine 0.0.74 silently strips unknown `network.mode` fields.
+
+```js
+import { SandboxManager, SandboxRuntimeConfigSchema } from '@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime';
+const capabilities = SandboxManager.getNetworkModeCapabilities?.();
+if (capabilities?.apiVersion !== 1 || !capabilities.modes.includes('restricted')) {
+  throw new Error('Required native network mode is unavailable');
+}
+await SandboxManager.initialize(SandboxRuntimeConfigSchema.parse(baseConfig), ask, false);
+const launch = await SandboxManager.wrapWithSandboxArgv(command, '/bin/bash', {
+  network: { mode: 'restricted' },
+});
+// Spawn launch.argv with shell:false and a caller-owned replacement environment.
+// Serialize initialize/update/wrap/cleanup/reset in the existing coordinator.
+```
+
+## Public contract
+
+`network.mode?: 'proxy' | 'restricted' | 'direct'` is present in the public
+schemas, inferred config types and `SandboxWrapConfig`. Only `network` gains a
+nested partial type at the wrap seam; existing filesystem override semantics are
+unchanged. Per-wrap fields take precedence over the initialized config; omitted
+fields (including explicitly `undefined` optional fields) inherit. Null/unknown
+modes are invalid. Validation normalizes the effective credentials and sibling
+network/top-level options before rendering; undefined cannot erase a restriction
+only during validation. A provided credentials object still replaces the whole
+credentials section, as before. A provided filesystem object keeps the existing
+special `disabled ?? false` rule; an absent filesystem object inherits it.
+An initialized/updateConfig mode is a default for *later* wraps.
+Omitting the mode everywhere retains the legacy platform and proxy behavior.
+Unknown explicit values throw, including when a caller does not parse the schema.
+
+`SandboxManager.getNetworkModeCapabilities()` returns a frozen
+`{apiVersion: 1, platform, modes}`. Only `platform: 'macos'` lists the three modes;
+Linux, Windows, and other platforms list none. This is implementation capability,
+not initialization health, runtime attestation, or authorization. Explicit modes
+on unsupported platforms fail before initialization/wrapping work. Legacy Linux
+and Windows routes remain unchanged, including their existing Unix limitations.
+
+Every explicit-mode wrap requires completed successful initialization, retained
+network context and a successful readiness result. `updateConfig()` and retained
+config after `reset()` are not readiness. Reset invalidates explicit-mode health
+at entry. Lifecycle calls must remain serialized; no new concurrent singleton
+lifecycle contract is introduced. Public `NetworkModeError.code` is one of
+`NETWORK_MODE_INVALID`, `NETWORK_MODE_UNSUPPORTED`, `NETWORK_MODE_INCOMPATIBLE`,
+`NETWORK_MODE_NOT_READY`; schema parsing itself reports ordinary Zod issues.
+
+## Native semantics (macOS)
+
+| Mode | IP egress (including private/loopback) | Bind / inbound | Unix sockets | Workload proxy |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| absent | unchanged legacy behavior | unchanged | unchanged | unchanged |
+| proxy | existing native proxy-only rules | existing explicit knobs | existing explicit knobs | initialized proxy/auth/CA/agent |
+| restricted | denied natively, TCP and UDP; no proxy port reachable | denied | denied | none |
+| direct | unrestricted native IP outbound, not a wildcard proxy | denied by default; `allowLocalBinding:true` explicitly admits all local IP addresses/ports and inbound | denied by default; existing `allowUnixSockets` / `allowAllUnixSockets` explicitly opt in | none |
+
+Direct uses `(remote ip "*:*")`, **not** `(allow network*)`. It includes IPv4,
+IPv6, mapped IPv4, loopback and private targets. The rule is IP-wide, not a
+TCP-only or host-scoped grant; it does not add raw-socket privileges the OS user
+lacks. UDP clients that bind (including Node's implicit pre-send bind) require
+`allowLocalBinding:true`; this also grants listening/inbound on **all interfaces**,
+not only loopback. Do not grant that knob merely because an endpoint needs UDP.
+TCP egress alone does not require this knob. Unix opt-in retains the existing
+path-subtree bind/connect semantics and filesystem checks; it is not an exact
+socket inode capability or a promise of Unix inbound/listener support. Arbitrary
+Unix IPC can delegate authority (SSH agents, Docker, etc.); keep it disabled by
+default. The native harness is the gate for each stated IP behavior, not profile
+text inspection. System DNS, trustd and TLS success are not proven by IP tests.
+
+Restricted emits explicit network and AF_INET/AF_INET6/AF_UNIX socket denies,
+without proxy/local/Unix exceptions. It retains the existing essential Mach,
+AF_SYSTEM, process, and filesystem baseline; this is no *application socket
+network*, not a proof against all OS-service covert channels or inherited file
+descriptors. Callers must not pass network descriptors or unsafe launch agents.
+
+## Config compatibility and host infrastructure
+
+All explicit modes reject `filesystem.disabled:true`; they reuse the complete
+existing read/write renderer, credential deny handling, defaults, and mandatory
+protections (with existing explicit filesystem options). No compiler is copied.
+
+Restricted rejects effective `allowLocalBinding`, `allowAllUnixSockets`, nonempty
+`allowUnixSockets`, additional `allowMachLookup`, `allowAppleEvents`, and
+`enableWeakerNetworkIsolation`. Direct also rejects additional Mach/AppleEvents/
+weaker-isolation permissions in this strict draft. Non-proxy modes reject masked
+credentials because proxy substitution would no longer occur; deny-only
+credentials remain supported.
+
+**Restricted can inherit initialized host `parentProxy`, proxy ports, TLS
+termination, filterRequest, allowedDomains and any deniedDomains.** They are
+*dormant host proxy policy*, not workload network grants in restricted mode.
+No proxy ports/auth, bridge, CA trust environment, JVM/SSH agent injection, or
+CA/agent filesystem read re-allows are passed to a non-proxy workload. In
+particular, Pi's connectGuard parentProxy plus hard-deny lists remain compatible
+with a mode-only restricted wrap and later policy restore. Restrictions are not
+silently erased to make a mode work. Effective native escape options must instead
+be removed explicitly from the per-attempt projection or rejected.
+
+Direct requires empty effective allowedDomains and deniedDomains, no
+filterRequest and no strictAllowlist: native whole-IP egress cannot honor these
+policies. It also rejects initialized host/domain policy even if a per-wrap
+empty array tries to erase it: a whole-network projection must first pass through
+`updateConfig`. It may inherit dormant parentProxy/TLS infrastructure, but
+deliberately bypasses that proxy. Clearing hard denies or converting a host-only grant to
+whole-IP authority is **not** an authorized translation. The Engine must first
+prove whole-network authority with no conflicting deny/delegation ceiling.
+
+Proxy still uses the initialized manager's live domain/request policy. Per-wrap
+mode selects the native profile, **not** a separate proxy policy or per-attempt
+proxy snapshot. A live `updateConfig` can affect existing proxy workloads exactly
+as before, but cannot expand an already-created restricted native profile.
+Callers must continue using the coordinator and freeze per-attempt authority at
+their own public policy seam. This patch does not implement that Engine change.
+
+## Environment, TLS, maintenance
+
+SRT does not sanitize an arbitrary caller environment. Non-proxy modes inject no
+proxy/CA/agent settings, but inherited values or user command code are not erased.
+Use a replacement environment and do not pass secrets/network descriptors.
+Strict TLS remains the default. No trustd grant, verification-disable flag,
+certificate-error inference, or automatic retry is added. System TLS is a later
+explicit startup-policy feature with its own conflict/risk review.
+
+This patch edits distributed JS and declarations, not unpublished upstream TS.
+Source-map directives on edited artifacts are removed because those maps describe
+the unpatched source; unchanged maps/files and LICENSE remain intact. Rebase and
+revalidate every touched distribution artifact on any future dependency upgrade.
+Remove only after an upstream public API meets this contract and passes equivalent
+native/API tests; until then, missing capability must fail closed. No upstream PR
+or publication is claimed.
